\chapter{Development stages}

Each stage is an edition of the project. At its boundary everything builds, the
tests pass, and the stage's ``proven when'' fact holds. No stage begins before
the previous one is proven.

\section{Stage 1 --- the walking skeleton}

The thinnest slice that runs. It touches every stage-one concept, however
thinly. The concepts are the rope model, one anchor (a bar), the closure, the
topology (the determinant only), a two-entry recogniser (unknot and trefoil),
the renderer, a React page with Tailwind and one lesson stub, and one lesson
record. Tooling is Vite, Vitest and the pinned libraries.

\paragraph{Decision gate.} Before the rest of stage one is built, the rigid
chain is measured for stability, for no-pass behaviour, and for frame cost. The
gate passed on 2026-10-19 with the rigid chain and a link speed cap. The soft-body
rope was measured and not used. The measurements and settings are recorded in
the rope decision of the architecture chapter.

\paragraph{Proven when} the page runs; a rope can be dragged with the mouse and
draws continuously; and a test that drives the rope into a trefoil returns
\texttt{trefoil}, while a test of the unknot returns \texttt{unknot}.

\paragraph{Status.} Proven on 2026-10-19. The page builds and runs in a headless
browser, and the rope can be dragged. A rope tied by hand into an overhand knot
is named \texttt{trefoil} by the page. The unit tests also feed the recogniser a
scripted curve, which covers the same knot without a person tying it.

\section{Stage 2 --- the recogniser}

Adds the Alexander polynomial, the full library for the lessons, anchors of
every type, and the build-time collision check.

\paragraph{Proven when} every library entry is recognised from a scripted rope
of that knot; every pair in the library is separated by the two invariants; and
a clove hitch on a bar is recognised as a link with the bar.

\section{Stage 3 --- guided mode for one knot}

Guided mode for overhand on a bight, with steps. The UI waits for the
recogniser to confirm each step.

\paragraph{Proven when} a guided lesson runs from its first step to the
recognised knot, driven by the recogniser and not by a timer.

\section{Stage 4 --- sandbox}

Free play. The recogniser names any library knot and says nothing otherwise.

\paragraph{Proven when} a rope tied into each library knot is named in sandbox
mode, and an unknown configuration is left unnamed.

\section{Stage 5 --- quiz mode}

The instruction is shown, the player ties the knot, and asks for an evaluation.

\paragraph{Proven when} a correct tie is marked correct; a wrong knot is marked
wrong and named as the knot the player actually tied; and an untied rope is not
evaluated.

\section{Stage 6 --- the knot catalogue}

Adds the lessons from the research, in teaching order. Anchoring: figure-eight
on a bight, clove hitch, girth hitch. Belaying: munter hitch, mule overhand.
Rappel: the stoppers and the rope joins, as decided in the analysis. Cleaning:
the rappel stoppers and backups, with no separate knot. Other: prusik and the
friction hitches, shape only.

\paragraph{Proven when} each lesson has a scripted reference rope that the
recogniser names, and each lesson cites its web sources.

\section{State of the work}

Lifecycle: planned, drafted, implemented, stable.

